## Architecture

### Two-phase encoding pipeline

1. **POST** `/api/sessions` — Client sends S3 config + optional webhook URL with Auth0 JWT
2. Service returns `{ sessionId, tusEndpoint, uploadToken, maxUploadSize }`
3. Client uploads source file via **tus protocol** to `/api/tus` using the upload token; supports resumable chunked uploads
4. On tus upload completion, API auto-probes the file with ffprobe and sets session status to `uploaded`
5. Client polls `GET /api/sessions/:id` to receive probe results — detected tracks (video and audio metadata)
6. Client computes suggested encoding config from probe results, user reviews and modifies (renditions, audio groups, copy/reencode toggles)
7. **POST** `/api/sessions/:id/encode` — Client submits final encoding config with Auth0 JWT
8. Session enters FIFO queue; webhook sent (if configured) with `queued` status + queue position
9. When session reaches front of queue, FFmpeg encodes to HLS with fMP4 segments and ABR variants
10. Progress webhooks sent during encoding (~every 5%), or client polls `GET /api/sessions/:id`
11. Output files uploaded to client-specified S3 bucket via MinIO client
12. Final webhook sent with `completed` status + list of S3 object keys + master playlist

### Media analysis (ProbeService)

- Wraps `ffprobe -v quiet -print_format json -show_format -show_streams`
- Detects video tracks (codec, resolution, bitrate, frame rate, profile, language, title)
- Detects audio tracks (codec, bitrate, channels, sample rate, language, title)
- Returns raw probe results to the client; encoding config suggestions are computed entirely in the frontend (`EncodeConfigForm.vue`)

### Encoding features

- **fMP4 segments**: HLS output uses fragmented MP4 (`.m4s` segments + `init.mp4` init segments) for lower overhead and CMAF compatibility
- **Copy mode** (`-c:v copy` / `-c:a copy`): Pass-through for pre-encoded streams, avoiding re-encoding
- **Audio groups**: Different audio quality tiers mapped to video renditions via HLS `#EXT-X-MEDIA` GROUP-ID
- **Multi-track audio**: Supports multiple audio tracks (e.g., different languages) with metadata preservation
- **Metadata**: Language and name attributes in `-var_stream_map` for HLS `#EXT-X-MEDIA` and `#EXT-X-STREAM-INF` tags
- **Channel control**: Mono/stereo/5.1/7.1 per audio group via `-ac`

### Upload resilience

- **Tus protocol**: Resumable, chunked uploads via `@tus/server` + `@tus/file-store` (backend) and `tus-js-client` (frontend, 50 MB chunks with automatic retries)
- **Upload expiration**: Incomplete uploads expire after 10 minutes; expired uploads cleaned up on shutdown via `TusUploadService.onModuleDestroy()`
- **Bearer auth on tus**: Every tus request is authenticated via the upload token (validated in `onIncomingRequest` hook)

### Web client flow

1. User signs in via Auth0 Universal Login (redirect flow)
2. User fills in S3 config and selects a file in `SessionConfigForm`
3. On submit, `App.vue` creates a session and uploads the file via tus (resumable, chunked)
4. On tus completion the API auto-probes the file; client polls until probe results are available
5. `EncodeConfigForm` computes a suggested encoding config from the probe results on mount; user reviews and configures encoding (renditions, audio groups, copy toggles)
6. User clicks "Start Encoding" — `App.vue` submits the encode config
7. Transitions to progress view; `useSessionPoller` polls the API every 2s
8. Polling auto-stops when session reaches a terminal status (`completed` or `failed`)
9. On completion, a Video.js player loads the HLS master playlist from S3

### FIFO queue

- One encoding job runs at a time
- Sessions processed in first-come-first-served order
- Queue position updates sent via webhook when positions shift

## API Endpoints

| Method | Path | Auth | Description |
|--------|------|------|-------------|
| POST | `/api/sessions` | Auth0 JWT | Create encoding session (S3 + webhook config) |
| ALL | `/api/tus` , `/api/tus/*` | Bearer (upload token) | Tus upload endpoint — resumable chunked file upload, auto-probes on completion |
| POST | `/api/sessions/:sessionId/encode` | Auth0 JWT | Submit encoding config, enqueue for processing |
| GET | `/api/sessions/:sessionId` | Auth0 JWT | Poll session status |
| DELETE | `/api/sessions/:sessionId` | Auth0 JWT | Cancel and delete session (`created`, `uploading`, or `uploaded` status) |

## Session Lifecycle

```
created -> uploading -> uploaded -> queued -> encoding -> uploading_to_s3 -> completed
                                                      \-> failed
```

## Key Conventions

- DTOs use `class-validator` decorators and are validated via NestJS `ValidationPipe` (whitelist + forbidNonWhitelisted)
- Webhook configuration is optional in `CreateSessionDto` — when omitted, no webhooks are sent; clients can poll instead
- FFmpeg args are built dynamically based on GPU availability, encode config (audio groups, copy mode, metadata)
- File uploads use the tus protocol (`@tus/server` + `@tus/file-store`) with 10 GB max size, mounted via `EncodeModule.onModuleInit()` on `/api/tus`
- Graceful shutdown enabled (`app.enableShutdownHooks()`); `QueueService`, `FfmpegService`, and `TusUploadService` implement `OnModuleDestroy` to clean up active processes and expired uploads
- Environment config via `dotenv`: `PORT`, `AUTH0_DOMAIN`, `AUTH0_AUDIENCE`, `WORK_DIR`, `FFMPEG_TIMEOUT_MS`, `CORS_ORIGIN`, `MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE` (API); `VITE_AUTH0_DOMAIN`, `VITE_AUTH0_CLIENT_ID`, `VITE_AUTH0_AUDIENCE`, `VITE_API_BASE_URL` (app)
- Swagger decorators on all DTOs and endpoints for auto-generated API docs
- Web client stores S3 config and encode configs (keyed by media layout fingerprint via `layoutStorage.ts`) in localStorage between sessions for convenience
- Video.js quality selector is a custom plugin (`videojs-quality-selector.ts`) using native ES6 classes — the `videojs-hls-quality-selector` npm package is incompatible with Video.js 8 (Babel `_inheritsLoose` cannot extend native ES6 classes)
- Vite config uses `resolve.dedupe: ['video.js']` to ensure a single Video.js instance across all modules

## GPU Detection

At startup, `FfmpegService.onModuleInit()` checks:
1. `nvidia-smi` is available
2. `ffmpeg -hwaccels` includes `cuda`

When GPU is available: `h264_nvenc` encoder, `scale_cuda` filter, `-hwaccel cuda`
When not: `libx264` encoder, standard `scale` filter

## Scripts

From the repo root:

- `npm run dev` — start both API and web client in dev mode (via `concurrently`)
- `npm -w api run start:dev` — API dev server with watch mode
- `npm -w api run build` — compile API to `api/dist/`
- `npm -w api run start:prod` — run compiled API output
- `npm -w api test` — unit tests
- `npm -w api run test:e2e` — end-to-end tests
- `npm -w app run dev` — web client dev server (Vite, port 5173)
- `npm -w app run build` — production build of the web client
